# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true, which checks text files out
# with CRLF line endings. CNAME must stay exactly 21 bytes: the domain
# and one \n, with no BOM and no trailing content. GitHub Pages reads
# this file as the source of truth for the custom domain, and a
# trailing \r makes it reject the name, which drops the site back to
# the github.io address and breaks every link to
# mohamedelhayyany.com until it is fixed and DNS settles.
#
# index.html, style.css and script.js are LF in the working tree. They
# were CRLF when this file was written; the STEP 47 edits rewrote them
# through tools that emit LF, and script.js came out mixed, so all
# three were normalised to LF rather than left half and half. They are
# deliberately NOT pinned here: autocrlf stores LF and checks CRLF back
# out, which is lossless either way and is what a Windows clone
# expects. Line endings do not affect how the page renders or how it
# opens from a double click.
CNAME text eol=lf

# No line-ending conversion on binaries, whatever git guesses them to
# be. A stray \r inserted into a PNG or a TTF corrupts the file.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.pdf  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.ico  binary
